sections.content:
Let us be precise about the pain before prescribing the cure. Consider this realistic antipattern:

```js
class Checkout {
  pay(amount, method, details) {
    if (method === 'stripe') {
      // 15 lines of Stripe SDK code
    } else if (method === 'paypal') {
      // 12 lines of PayPal SDK code
    } else if (method === 'crypto') {
      // 18 lines of Web3 code
    } else {
      throw new Error('Unknown payment method');
    }
  }
}
```

This violates at least three design principles:

**Open/Closed Principle (OCP):** The class must be *opened* (modified) every time a new payment method is added, risking regression in the existing branches.

**Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):** `Checkout` now knows the internals of Stripe, PayPal, and crypto. Its reasons to change multiply — a Stripe SDK upgrade, a PayPal endpoint change, or a new blockchain network all force changes to a class whose responsibility should be managing an order.

**Testability:** To test the Stripe path, you must instantiate `Checkout` and pass `method='stripe'`. You cannot test Stripe logic in isolation without constructing the entire Checkout context. Mocking requires intercepting inside a monolithic method.

The Strategy pattern surgically removes the algorithm branches from the Context and distributes them across separate, focused classes. The result is a thin Context that orchestrates, and fat-free strategies that compute.

sections.code:
// ── BEFORE: monolithic if/else antipattern ───────────────────────
class CheckoutBefore {
  pay(amount, method, details) {
    if (method === 'stripe') {
      console.log(`[Stripe] Charging $${amount} to card *${details.last4}`);
      return { success: true, provider: 'stripe' };
    } else if (method === 'paypal') {
      console.log(`[PayPal] Billing ${details.email} for $${amount}`);
      return { success: true, provider: 'paypal' };
    } else if (method === 'crypto') {
      console.log(`[Crypto] Sending ${amount} ETH to ${details.wallet}`);
      return { success: true, provider: 'crypto' };
    }
    throw new Error(`Unknown method: ${method}`);
  }
}

// ── AFTER: Strategy pattern ──────────────────────────────────────

// ConcreteStrategy A
const StripeStrategy = {
  execute({ amount, details }) {
    console.log(`[Stripe] Charging $${amount} to card *${details.last4}`);
    return { success: true, provider: 'stripe', amount };
  }
};

// ConcreteStrategy B
const PayPalStrategy = {
  execute({ amount, details }) {
    console.log(`[PayPal] Billing ${details.email} for $${amount}`);
    return { success: true, provider: 'paypal', amount };
  }
};

// ConcreteStrategy C
const CryptoStrategy = {
  execute({ amount, details }) {
    console.log(`[Crypto] Sending ${amount} ETH to ${details.wallet}`);
    return { success: true, provider: 'crypto', amount };
  }
};

// ConcreteStrategy D — added with ZERO changes to Checkout
const KlarnaStrategy = {
  execute({ amount, details }) {
    console.log(`[Klarna] BNPL for ${details.email}, total $${amount}`);
    return { success: true, provider: 'klarna', amount };
  }
};

// Context — knows nothing about how payment works
class Checkout {
  constructor(strategy) {
    this._strategy = strategy;
  }

  setStrategy(strategy) {
    this._strategy = strategy; // runtime swap
  }

  pay(amount, details) {
    if (!this._strategy) throw new Error('No payment strategy configured');
    // Pre-delegation logic (logging, validation) stays here
    console.log(`Processing order of $${amount}...`);
    return this._strategy.execute({ amount, details });
  }
}

// ── Usage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const checkout = new Checkout(StripeStrategy);
checkout.pay(99.99, { last4: '4242' });
// Processing order of $99.99...
// [Stripe] Charging $99.99 to card *4242

checkout.setStrategy(PayPalStrategy); // switch at runtime
checkout.pay(49.00, { email: 'user@example.com' });
// [PayPal] Billing user@example.com for $49

checkout.setStrategy(KlarnaStrategy); // NEW — no Checkout changes
checkout.pay(299.00, { email: 'vip@example.com' });
// [Klarna] BNPL for vip@example.com, total $299

sections.content:
A critical practical question: **who decides which strategy to use, and when?**

**Constructor injection** is the most common approach. The strategy is provided when the Context is created, often by a factory or a DI container:

```js
function createCheckout(providerName) {
  const strategies = {
    stripe: StripeStrategy,
    paypal: PayPalStrategy,
    crypto: CryptoStrategy,
  };
  const strategy = strategies[providerName];
  if (!strategy) throw new Error(`Unknown provider: ${providerName}`);
  return new Checkout(strategy);
}

const checkout = createCheckout(process.env.PAYMENT_PROVIDER);
```

This pattern is common in server applications: the strategy is read from configuration at startup. The rest of the application code never sees payment provider names — it works with the Checkout instance and calls `pay()`.

**Setter injection (runtime swap)** is used when the algorithm must change mid-session. In a checkout UI, the user might switch from credit card to PayPal after adding items to the cart. `checkout.setStrategy(PayPalStrategy)` handles this cleanly.

**Function-based strategies** are idiomatic in JavaScript. Instead of objects with an `execute()` method, a strategy can simply be a function: `checkout = new Checkout((data) => stripeSDK.charge(data))`. This is useful for one-off or anonymous strategies. The Context calls `this._strategy(data)` rather than `this._strategy.execute(data)`.

**DI containers** (Inversify, NestJS DI, Angular injectors) automate strategy registration and selection. In NestJS, you might define a `PAYMENT_STRATEGY` injection token and bind it to the appropriate class based on environment or request context. The Context simply declares `@Inject(PAYMENT_STRATEGY) private strategy: PaymentStrategy` — the container handles the rest.

sections.code:
// ── Strategy as a plain function (JS idiom) ──────────────────────
class Sorter {
  constructor(compareFn) {
    this._compareFn = compareFn;
  }

  setStrategy(compareFn) {
    this._compareFn = compareFn;
  }

  sort(items) {
    return [...items].sort(this._compareFn); // non-mutating
  }
}

// Concrete strategies as simple functions
const byPriceAsc  = (a, b) => a.price - b.price;
const byPriceDesc = (a, b) => b.price - a.price;
const byName      = (a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
const byRating    = (a, b) => b.rating - a.rating; // highest first

const products = [
  { name: 'Laptop', price: 999, rating: 4.5 },
  { name: 'Mouse',  price: 29,  rating: 4.8 },
  { name: 'Desk',   price: 349, rating: 3.9 },
  { name: 'Chair',  price: 499, rating: 4.2 },
];

const sorter = new Sorter(byPriceAsc);
console.log(sorter.sort(products).map(p => p.name));
// ['Mouse', 'Desk', 'Chair', 'Laptop']

sorter.setStrategy(byRating);
console.log(sorter.sort(products).map(p => p.name));
// ['Mouse', 'Laptop', 'Chair', 'Desk']

// ── Strategy selected dynamically from user input ─────────────────
function getSortStrategy(field, direction = 'asc') {
  const strategies = {
    'price-asc':   byPriceAsc,
    'price-desc':  byPriceDesc,
    'name-asc':    byName,
    'rating-desc': byRating,
  };
  return strategies[`${field}-${direction}`] ?? byPriceAsc;
}

// Simulate dropdown selection
const userChoice = 'rating-desc';
sorter.setStrategy(getSortStrategy(...userChoice.split('-')));
console.log(sorter.sort(products).map(p => p.name));
// ['Mouse', 'Laptop', 'Chair', 'Desk']

